## Dark Mode — Every Element Must Have Both Variants

This app supports dark and light mode via `useTheme()`. Every visual element must work in both.

### Rules

- **Every background, text, and border class MUST have a `dark:` variant.** No exceptions.
- **Use `theme === 'dark'` ternaries** for complex class switching (not separate components for dark/light).
- **Never use raw `bg-white` or `text-black`** without a `dark:bg-gray-*` / `dark:text-gray-*` companion.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — no dark variant
<div className="bg-white border border-gray-200 text-gray-900">

// ✅ GOOD
<div className="bg-white dark:bg-gray-900 border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">

// ✅ GOOD — ternary for complex themes
<div className={theme === 'dark' ? 'bg-gray-800 text-white' : 'bg-white text-gray-900'}>
```

### Common pairs (use these, not custom values)

| Light | Dark | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| `bg-white` | `dark:bg-gray-900` | Page/card backgrounds |
| `bg-gray-50` | `dark:bg-gray-800` | Inset/secondary backgrounds |
| `text-gray-900` | `dark:text-gray-100` | Primary text |
| `text-gray-600` | `dark:text-gray-400` | Secondary text |
| `text-gray-500` | `dark:text-gray-400` | Muted/caption text |
| `border-gray-200` | `dark:border-gray-700` | Borders and dividers |
| `bg-gray-100` | `dark:bg-gray-700` | Hover states, subtle fills |

### Brand colors

Brand colors (`bg-brand-mint`, `text-teal-*`) generally work in both modes. But always verify contrast — teal on dark gray needs different opacity than teal on white.

